1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.769
In this lesson, you will learn how to run

2
00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.472
your agent's code safely in the cloud

3
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:07.774
using E2B sandbox environment.

4
00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:11.177
You'll create, manage, and interact with a remote environment

5
00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.480
that execute code securely.

6
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.483
All right, let's go. In this lab,

7
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:19.486
we'll equip our agent to write code safely

8
00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:21.788
and scalably inside a sandbox using E2B.

9
00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:27.093
provides a very simple and easy-to-use APIs to manage sandboxes.

10
00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.130
You can create a sandbox by importing

11
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.799
the Sandbox class from the E2B Python package.

12
00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.101
You can set timeout in seconds,

13
00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:37.303
so in this case, the sandbox will be

14
00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:39.606
alive for 1 hour, and we can run code.

15
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:43.743
by passing it as a string to the run_code method.

16
00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.046
We're getting back an Execution object

17
00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.748
and you can think about it as a notebook cell output.

18
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.351
In this case, the code is just printing hello world

19
00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.953
and you can see it inside the Logs object

20
00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:56.956
inside the Execution that we're getting from the Sandbox.

21
00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.193
If you were to return a variable,

22
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:03.963
the results will be inside the Results array in the Execution object.

23
00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.766
E2B supports multiple languages.

24
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.769
It defaults to Python, but you can always change it,

25
00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.471
in this case to javascript,

26
00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.006
and we can run the same

27
00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:14.774
hello world printing that we did previously.

28
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.445
And we have it, in the logs as well.

29
00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.146
We can also create a plot

30
00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:23.049
by running Python code that use matplotlib

31
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.651
to create the plot.

32
00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.320
and we get it back in the

33
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.422
execution. In order to see our plot,

34
00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.827
we can import some Jupyter function and display it in the cell.

35
00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.529
Sometimes it's useful to list all the sandboxes

36
00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.232
you are using. In order to do so,

37
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:41.101
you call the .list method on the

38
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:43.236
Sandbox class and you get the next page.

39
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.472
Here we can see all the

40
00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:47.540
IDs of the sandboxes that are live.

41
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.211
We can also print uh some useful fields about the sandbox,

42
00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.214
like the metadata, its ID,

43
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:55.882
and when it was created and

44
00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.717
the template that it was created from.

45
00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:01.721
You can query sandboxes by attaching some metadata

46
00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:04.224
upon creation.

47
00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:07.093
In this case, we are setting the name to be find me.

48
00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:10.897
And when we list them, we are passing a SandboxQuery,

49
00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.600
imported from the e2b Python package,

50
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.603
with the same name and also getting

51
00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.605
only the running ones.

52
00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.009
And here we have found one sandbox with that ID.

53
00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:25.745
E2B sandboxes have a file system.

54
00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.948
We can create a directory by calling the

55
00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.850
files.make_dir method.

56
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.052
Here we are creating a data directory

57
00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.955
under the main user folder in the sandbox.

58
00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.125
we can write some content to a file.

59
00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:42.729
In this case, we are writing Hello from the sandbox

60
00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:46.432
inside a hello.txt file in the directory we just created.

61
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.403
We can read back the content

62
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:54.007
by using the .read method. Here we have it.

63
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:57.010
And finally, we can remove this file.

64
00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:03.650
Since sandboxes can die or time out during the labs.

65
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.853
We have created a useful function called create_sandbox

66
00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:10.356
that it will create a sandbox if it doesn't exist,

67
00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.660
and when you call it again, it will reconnect to it

68
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.862
if it has not died.

69
00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:18.565
Sandboxes are general compute.

70
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:21.568
We can, for example, run a website in them. It's straightforward.

71
00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:24.070
We just need to create an index.html file

72
00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.372
and spawn the the server.

73
00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.108
to serve that file.

74
00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.711
So, first, we reconnect to the sandbox,

75
00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.114
we write to index.html inside it,

76
00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:38.418
and a very simple HTML file.

77
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.723
Then we can use the http.server Python module

78
00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:46.726
to spawn a server on port 3000

79
00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:50.997
and get the host of the sandbox from the same port.

80
00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.399
To visualize the website,

81
00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.202
we can use the IFrame class

82
00:03:56.202 --> 00:04:00.006
in the IPython.display function in the notebook, passing the host.

83
00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.208
And here we can see the website.

84
00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:07.146
we just created from within the sandbox in the cell.

85
00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:11.351
We now know how to use E2B sandbox.

86
00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:14.053
So it's time to equip our agent

87
00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.356
with the ability of running code in the cloud.

88
00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:19.626
We are modifying the execute_code function

89
00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:21.628
that we created in the previous lab.

90
00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:25.031
and this time we are passing to the sandbox run_code

91
00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:27.033
the code generated by the language model.

92
00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.770
and we are recreating our tool registry.

93
00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.274
So let's import the execute_code_schema,

94
00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:36.075
the agent, OpenAI.

95
00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:39.078
and create the client and the sandbox

96
00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.781
in order to run our agent

97
00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.084
with ability of creating code in the cloud.

98
00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:48.821
We change a little bit the coding_agent function to have a nicer

99
00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.823
output using the log function.

100
00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.725
And this is why we are importing

101
00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:54.627
it and using it in the cell.

102
00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:57.830
So, let's try to run it and see

103
00:04:57.964 --> 00:05:00.933
the agent connecting to the sandbox,

104
00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.936
executing code on it,

105
00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:06.939
and giving us back the reply.

106
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.742
Here, our task was to make and run a function

107
00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.444
to roll a six faces dice.

108
00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:14.747
And the agent is saying I created a function called roll_dice,

109
00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:18.151
and we can actually see the code that it created

110
00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:20.153
and run on the sandbox.

111
00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.455
And also, it simulated rolling.

112
00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:25.258
and the result was true.

113
00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:27.327
Let's ask our agent to work

114
00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:29.128
a little bit with the file system.

115
00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:32.732
And remember, we still don't have file system tools

116
00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.434
for this agent here,

117
00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.736
but by executing Python code,

118
00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:39.138
it can actually create and edit files as well.

119
00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.909
So let's ask to create a file.txt with hello world inside it,

120
00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:45.311
read it, and print it back to us its content.

121
00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:49.816
We can see the agent running code.

122
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:54.220
creating the file and then printing back to us.

123
00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:58.558
We can actually see the code here with the open Python function

124
00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:02.462
is writing the file to the file system. By just

125
00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:05.631
having the ability to execute Python code in the sandbox,

126
00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:07.834
the agent is really quite powerful.

127
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.902
It can create and even remove files.

128
00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.004
Let's test its abilities

129
00:06:12.105 --> 00:06:14.507
by tasking it to create a simple snake game

130
00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.709
as a web page so we can play.

131
00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:20.480
Here, we are giving the agent a more specific query,

132
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.281
in which we tell all the

133
00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:24.183
features we want the game to have.

134
00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:27.687
Um, we are telling that it has to be just JS

135
00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:29.989
and no other framework such as jQuery.

136
00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.692
We want a 10x10 grid and arrow keys

137
00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.727
for movement and some other features.

138
00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:38.231
So, the agent has created the file.

139
00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:41.234
We can now reopen it using the

140
00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:44.237
IFrame notebook class.

141
00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:48.241
We can now see it by running the Python HTTP server.

142
00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:52.245
Okay, let me try to play.

143
00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.747
So I can move my snake with the arrow keys,

144
00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:56.215
I can eat a fruit, and

145
00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:57.950
I can grow when I do so.

146
00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:00.253
And if I hit a wall, I die.

147
00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:02.255
and I can restart my game.

148
00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:05.291
by pressing the restart button. Super.

149
00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:07.693
Since the agent create an HTML file,

150
00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:09.595
we can read it, store it,

151
00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:11.697
and then open it in our browser.

152
00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:14.000
Here, I'm opening the HTML file.

153
00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:17.370
in a browser, in my computer, and I can just play

154
00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.072
to the game in the same way we did on the sandbox.

155
00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:24.477
Since we're using GPT-5 to generate this game,

156
00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:27.046
Every game will be slightly different.

157
00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:28.815
So you will probably get something that

158
00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:30.550
looks similar to what I'm playing here.

159
00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.953
Great. So our agent can now execute code in the sandbox,

160
00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.755
secure and scalable environment.

161
00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:38.658
In the next lab, we'll create a data analyst agent

162
00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:40.760
to analyze uploaded CSV files.

163
00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.262
All right, see you there.
